=== SIGNATURE ===
exlookup(lookup_value, lookup_array, return_array, [if_not_found], [match_mode], [search_mode])

=== COMMENT ===
Case-sensitive XLOOKUP: same arguments, same results, except text must match case exactly (via EXACT). match_mode 0 (default) exact; 2 wildcard (* ? ~, case-sensitive); -1 / 1 take an exact case-sensitive hit first, else XLOOKUP's next-smaller / next-larger. search_mode 1 (default) or -1 for last-to-first.

=== CODE ===
=LAMBDA(lookup_value, lookup_array, return_array, [if_not_found], [match_mode], [search_mode],
  LET(
    mode, z_opt(0, match_mode),
    smode_raw, z_opt(1, search_mode),
    smode, IF(ABS(smode_raw) = 2, SIGN(smode_raw), smode_raw),
    not_found, IF(ISOMITTED(if_not_found), NA(), if_not_found),
    one, LAMBDA(val,
      LET(
        hits, IFERROR(IF(mode = 2, REGEXTEST(lookup_array, z_wildcard_regex(val), 0), EXACT(lookup_array, val)), FALSE),
        IF(OR(hits),
          XLOOKUP(TRUE, hits, return_array, not_found, 0, smode),
          IF(OR(mode = -1, mode = 1),
            XLOOKUP(val, lookup_array, return_array, not_found, mode, smode),
            not_found)))),
    IF(ROWS(lookup_value) * COLUMNS(lookup_value) = 1, one(lookup_value), MAP(lookup_value, one))
  )
)

=== DESCRIPTION ===
XLOOKUP that is case-sensitive. Built-in XLOOKUP treats "Bat" and "bat" as the same key; exlookup only matches text whose case is identical, using EXACT. Everything else - argument order, defaults, spilling a multi-column return, #N/A when nothing matches - follows XLOOKUP.

Arguments:
  lookup_value   - the value to find. May be an array of values (see below).
  lookup_array   - the row or column to search.
  return_array   - the row(s)/column(s) to return; same length as lookup_array.
  [if_not_found] - returned when nothing matches (default #N/A, like XLOOKUP).
  [match_mode]   - 0  exact, case-sensitive (default).
                   2  wildcard, case-sensitive: * any run, ? one character, ~ escapes (~*, ~?, ~~), as in XLOOKUP. Translated to a regular expression by z_wildcard_regex.
                  -1 / 1  an exact case-sensitive match wins if there is one; otherwise XLOOKUP's own next-smaller / next-larger match (which, for text, uses Excel's case-insensitive ordering).
  [search_mode]  - 1 first-to-last (default), -1 last-to-first. The binary modes 2 / -2 are treated as 1 / -1 (the case-sensitive test is a scan, not a sorted search).

How it works: it builds a TRUE/FALSE mask of case-exact hits (EXACT, or REGEXTEST in wildcard mode) and asks XLOOKUP for the first TRUE, so the return shape, if_not_found and search direction all behave exactly as in XLOOKUP. Error cells in lookup_array count as no match.

Array lookup values: an array of lookup values is handled element-wise (MAP), so return_array must be a single row or column in that case - a multi-column return per lookup value cannot be spilled and gives #CALC!.

Numbers: EXACT compares text forms, so the number 2 matches the text "2" (XLOOKUP would not). Numbers against numbers behave as in XLOOKUP.

Single-value arguments (flags, counts, delimiters, a single lookup value) may arrive as 1x1 arrays (TOCOL(x), {x}, a one-cell FILTER/INDEX result); they are collapsed to true scalars with z_scalar, so they behave exactly like the plain value.

Optional arguments: a blank cell or "" behaves like omitting the argument (the default applies), and a number given as text ("2") is accepted as the number (z_opt).

Returns:
  The matching return_array row/column (a scalar for a single-column return), if_not_found / #N/A when nothing matches.

Example:
  exlookup("b", {"A";"B";"b";"C"}, {1;2;3;4})          ->  3      (XLOOKUP gives 2)
  exlookup("c", {"A";"B";"C"}, {1;2;3}, "none")         ->  "none"
  exlookup("b*t", {"Bat";"bat";"cat"}, {1;2;3}, , 2)    ->  2      (wildcard, case-sensitive)
  exlookup("b", {"b";"B";"b"}, {1;2;3}, , , -1)         ->  3      (last-to-first)
